# Portability (ANS-oriented)

## Cell and address units

- A **cell** is the natural stack integer; size is **implementation-defined**.
- Use `cell`, `cells`, `cell+`, `aligned`, `align`, not hard-coded `2*` or `4` for scaling indices unless the project targets one known platform.

```forth
\ BAD on unknown cell size
: field@ ( addr -- x ) 2* + @ ;

\ GOOD
: field@ ( addr -- x ) cell+ @ ;
```

## Char vs address units

Use `char`, `chars`, `char+` for character buffers; do not assume 1 char = 1 address unit on all systems.

## Stacks are stacks

Parameter and return stacks are **LIFO**. Do not assume you can treat the data stack as a random-access array in portable code (see `PICK` / `ROLL` in anti-patterns).

## Standard words first

Prefer CORE / CORE-EXT words from ANS before vendor extensions. If you must use a non-standard word, document it in the file header **Environmental dependencies**.

## Double numbers

See **`forth-numeric.mdc`** for `s>d`, `*/`, `m*`, pictured output. Each `d` on the stack is two cells deep.

## Throw / catch

Use `throw` with standardized or project-assigned codes; document which inputs cause which `ior` or exception.

## File and memory words

`allocate` / `free`, `read-file`, `write-file`, `open-file` — check `ior` on every call; pass to `throw` in application code. See `forth-memory.mdc` and `forth-io.mdc` for idioms.

## Alignment

Use `aligned` / `align` when mixing `char+` addressing with cell `@` / `!` — unaligned access may fail or be slow on some CPUs. See `forth-memory.mdc`.

## Standard vs Gforth extensions

Prefer standard words where easy; use Gforth extensions when the project targets Gforth only or when `compat/` shims keep the program standard-compliant. Many extensions have drop-in compat files — include them with portable code rather than reimplementing.

**Why embedded Forths omit extensions:** implementation cost (locals, FILE, FP, structured exceptions) vs Flash/RAM — see **`docs/FORTH-FEATURE-COMPLEXITY.md`** for a feature-by-feature complexity guide.

Before claiming portability, run:

```forth
\ batch: gforth ans-report.fs myprog.fs -e "print-ans-report bye"
include ans-report.fs
include myprog.fs
print-ans-report
```

`ans-report.fs` lists wordsets used and non-standard words; it does **not** detect non-standard *usage* of standard words.

## Environmental queries

Probe the host at run time instead of hard-coding cell size or wordset presence:

```forth
: need-file? ( -- )
  s" file" environment? abort" need FILE wordset" ;

s" address-unit-bits" environment? .   \ e.g. 32 or 64
s" gforth" environment? type cr       \ version string when true
```

Document required wordsets in the file header **Environmental dependencies**.

## Common throw codes (Gforth / ANS)

Know these when checking `ior` or reading backtraces:

| Code | Typical cause |
|------|----------------|
| `-4` | Stack underflow (often caught by text interpreter) |
| `-3` | Stack overflow |
| `-5` | Return stack overflow |
| `-6` | Return stack underflow |
| `-8` | Dictionary overflow |
| `-9` | Invalid memory address |
| `-10` | Division by zero |
| `-11` | Result out of range |
| `-12` | Argument type mismatch (control flow, `restore-input`, …) |
| `-13` | Undefined word |
| `-16` | Zero-length name (`create` with empty parse) |
| `-17` | Pictured numeric output overflow |
| `-19` | Word name too long |
| `-23` | Address alignment exception |
| `-32` | Invalid name argument (`to` on non-`value`) |

`gforth-fast` may silently produce wrong results on some arithmetic edge cases; use `gforth` while debugging.

## Legacy blocks

Block files (`block`, `load`, 1024-byte sectors) are pre-OS Forth mass storage. On modern systems use **`include` / `require` and files** — see `forth-io.mdc`. Use blocks only when maintaining legacy block-based codebases.
